<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lüshunkou</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Port Arthur, Ryojun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lüshunkou, known to Europeans as Port Arthur, is a port at the tip of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> peninsula in north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> leased it from the Qing in 1898 as an ice-free naval base, prompting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to take Weihaiwei. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> captured it after a long siege in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a> of 1904–1905 and held it as part of the Kwantung Leased Territory until 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lushunkou.jpg" alt="Lüshunkou" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CEphoto, Uwe Aranas · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L%C3%BCshunkouPrison-EntranceGate.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Russo-Japanese War of 1904–1905 Russia lost both its Pacific squadron at <b>Port Arthur</b> and the Baltic Fleet, annihilated at the Battle of Tsushima in May 1905 after a voyage of some 18,000 nautical miles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After capturing <b>Lüshunkou</b>, known to Europeans as Port Arthur, advance troops of the Japanese Second Army began three days of killing in the city.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-21">21 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>By the Treaty of Portsmouth in 1905 Japan took over Russia's lease of Kwantung Oblast, including <b>Port Arthur</b> (Ryojun) and Dalny (Dairen), together with the railway south of Changchun.</blockquote><a href="/state/kwantung-leased-territory">Kwantung Leased Territory</a></li><li><blockquote>Russia fortified <b>Port Arthur</b> as an ice-free Pacific naval base and built a commercial port at Dalny.</blockquote><a href="/state/kwantung-oblast">Kwantung Oblast</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1898, after Russia leased <b>Port Arthur</b>, Britain obtained Weihaiwei from the Qing Empire on a lease to last as long as Russia held Port Arthur.</blockquote><a href="/state/weihaiwei">Weihaiwei</a></li></ul></section>
